Which one should you choose?
Off The Cloud if you want a private place for your family and friends that nobody else owns, with your photos and files backed up at home on the same small device.
Instagram or Facebook if you want to reach a wide audience or follow public accounts: they're free and everyone is already there, in exchange for ads, a ranked feed and public posts used to train Meta's AI unless you object.
WhatsApp if you mainly want to chat: messages and calls, with photos that arrive in a chat rather than an album and live only on the phones.
FamilyAlbum if you want a family album with nothing to set up: it's free with ads, keeps your photos in its own cloud, and its paid plan adds longer videos.
Pixelfed if you want a public, ad-free Instagram alternative on the fediverse, and are happy to join a server or run one yourself.
Bringing your family in
Set up the device. The computer app prepares the Raspberry Pi 5's card, and you finish the setup from your phone over Bluetooth, in about 20 minutes. It asks for a bridge account, which gives your device its off-the.cloud address.
Add the family. In the web app, under Settings › Users, add an account for each person with Request bridge access ticked, and choose a first password to give them. They change it in Settings › Change Password, and from then on only they know it.
Add friends. In Friends, type the name of a friend's device, such as casa for casa.off-the.cloud, and send the request. Once they accept, your posts appear in each other's feed.
Share with everyone else. Choose photos in Images, add them to a collection and share it as a link.
